The self-diagnosing assertion (shipped one PR earlier) turned the third
owner-fired cron into the mechanism reading: toml analyzer RAN (1 file,
3 nodes), the mycli file node reached the map WITH entry_point and
entry_role but WITHOUT a `concepts` key, and the entrypoints list was
entirely empty — concept enrichment attached NOTHING in that process.
Local ground truth on the identical fixture: concepts present,
cli_command + main_function entrypoints derived.

MECHANISM. _PATTERN_CACHE was keyed on framework_id alone while
get_frameworks_dir() — half the function's input — is patchable. Six
tests in test_framework_patterns.py patch the dir; under the patch the
convention ids (main-functions, config-conventions, ...) cache as None.
The file's own autouse fixture — whose docstring NAMES this exact leak —
cleared the cache before each test in THAT file only, so the last
dir-patching test in an xdist worker left the poison for every other
file's tests on the worker. enrich_symbols then loaded None for every
convention pattern: zero concepts, zero concept-derived entrypoints,
`assert []`.

Expression depends on WORKER COMPOSITION — which tests precede the
victim — which is why the failure tracked pipeline shape and spent three
cron experiments looking like an interpreter problem (killed: nightly's
3.11 leg passes), then a container problem, then an install-set problem
(killed: the parity cron ran with the meta-package demonstrably
installed and failed identically), while every local reproduction —
single test, whole core suite, cross-package interleave — passed under
a different composition.

FIX. The cache key becomes (str(get_frameworks_dir()), framework_id): a
patched dir caches under its own key and cannot shadow the real
patterns — poisoning is impossible by construction for the dir-patching
shape. Regression tests pin both halves (patched-dir Nones must not
shadow the real patterns; caching still works within the patched scope).
The file-local fixture stays as hygiene, its docstring corrected to say
what it actually protected.

